    <item>
      <title>How do you actually self-host securely?</title>
      <link>https://lobste.rs/s/m43bas/how_do_you_actually_self_host_securely</link>
      <guid>https://lobste.rs/s/m43bas</guid>
      <author> by pralkarz</author>
      <pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:01:19 -0600</pubDate>
      <comments>https://lobste.rs/s/m43bas/how_do_you_actually_self_host_securely</comments>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I recognize that the title is rather vague, but I'm completely green when it comes to self-hosting, so I figured here would be a good place to ask for some pointers given the discussions I sometimes see happening in the comments. I tried researching first through DDG and Google, but (and I recognize this might be a skill issue) most things I found are either SEO slop or trying to sell/market some service.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For some more context and to give a concrete example as to what I'm interested in, I got my hands on a Raspberry Pi which I'd like to use to start tinkering with self-hosting. My main goal would be to host my (static) website on it, potentially adding more things in the future.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Some people I asked this question recommended solutions like Cloudflare Tunnels to make it secure, but using such a provider almost feels like it defeats the point of self-hosting, so I'm curious whether it's viable to do it using open source tooling, any gotchas one might encounter, and generally any thoughts you might have on the topic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(Also, feel free to suggest a tag other than &lt;code&gt;devops&lt;/code&gt;, I figured &lt;code&gt;security&lt;/code&gt; doesn't quite apply since it's a question and not a write-up/resource regarding security.)&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
      <category>devops</category>
      <category>ask</category>
    </item>
